00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.799
Part 4. You will hear part of an environmental science lecture about the impact of birds

00:00:34.799 --> 00:00:38.799
of prey on a farming community in Africa. First, you have some time to look at questions

00:00:38.799 --> 00:00:39.799
31 to 40.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:26.800
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:33.839
We've been looking at different types of conflicts that may arise between wildlife

00:01:33.839 --> 00:01:40.239
and humans at the boundaries of protected areas such as national parks and animal sanctuaries.

00:01:40.239 --> 00:01:45.040
I'd like to illustrate this by telling you about some research that I've been involved

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:51.160
in recently in the Central African country of Zambia in the area around the Chembe Bird

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:57.080
Sanctuary which contains over 300 of the listed birds of Zambia.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:03.879
These include a number of birds of prey such as eagles, hawks and owls that live by hunting

00:02:03.879 --> 00:02:07.040
and killing other birds and animals.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.160
Now most of the people living in the local communities near to the bird sanctuary are

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:19.279
small scale farmers and these birds of prey provide important social and ecological benefits

00:02:19.279 --> 00:02:20.679
to them.

00:02:20.679 --> 00:02:26.720
For example, a lot of damage can be caused to farmers' crops by rodents such as rats

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.800
which would consume the crops as they grow in the fields as well as after harvesting

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.399
if they weren't hunted and killed by the birds.

00:02:35.399 --> 00:02:40.880
And the predatory habits of these birds also protect farmers in other ways.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:47.839
For example, a major danger to rural workers is snakes whose bite may be dangerous or even

00:02:47.839 --> 00:02:55.160
fatal and birds of prey have a major role in keeping their populations under control.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:00.880
Local people have always been aware of these benefits and for years, even before the sanctuary

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:07.679
was opened in 1973, the birds played a key role in the culture of the region.

00:03:07.679 --> 00:03:14.679
However, more recently, the sanctuary and its birds have also become increasingly important

00:03:14.679 --> 00:03:22.080
to the community in economic terms because at present, after a relatively slow start,

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.639
tourism has become an important source of revenue for them.

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:32.399
However, although these birds of prey are protected by the government, their numbers

00:03:32.399 --> 00:03:34.119
are falling.

00:03:34.119 --> 00:03:36.399
Some of these deaths are accidental.

00:03:36.399 --> 00:03:42.000
Fatalities occur when birds alight on roads to catch and eat their prey and are hit by

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.240
fast-moving traffic.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:50.479
Drivers in Zambia have to take special care at night as birds may regard the quieter roads

00:03:50.479 --> 00:03:53.119
as safe places to sleep.

00:03:53.119 --> 00:03:59.240
Accidental deaths may also occur if these birds fly close to high-voltage power lines

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.759
as they may be electrocuted.

00:04:01.759 --> 00:04:07.080
This is a particular danger in the heavy rain which can occur in the region in the months

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.160
from December to April.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.600
And local farmers also pose a threat to these birds.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:20.600
As well as growing crops, small-scale farmers in the area also rear chickens.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:27.079
These provide food for the farmers' families as well as being an important source of income.

00:04:27.079 --> 00:04:33.880
But they're also an easy target for birds of prey and so farmers may shoot these birds,

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:40.040
which is illegal but understandable, or they may poison the birds, which again is illegal

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:48.359
and can have negative effects on the ecosystem.

00:04:48.359 --> 00:04:53.880
So how else can farmers protect their chickens from birds of prey?

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.839
Some people believe that to prevent the predators from settling near the area where the chickens

00:04:58.839 --> 00:05:03.959
are kept, it's best to keep this area free from vegetation.

00:05:03.959 --> 00:05:09.440
But in fact, this is counterproductive as it means the chickens have no cover to hide

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.320
in and they'll be easier for the birds to see.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:18.519
Another possibility would be to prevent the chickens from going outside at all and to

00:05:18.519 --> 00:05:22.440
keep them safe from predators inside a building.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:27.559
But this would cost far too much to be a practical solution.

00:05:27.559 --> 00:05:33.640
Nearly all the farmers reported that they spent a lot of time and effort trying to frighten

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:38.040
off the birds of prey without actually harming them.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:43.359
Most of the farmers had at least one dog and said this was a big help at scaring away

00:05:43.359 --> 00:05:45.440
the predators.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.559
Some of the farmers also reported that during the breeding season, when the chickens were

00:05:50.559 --> 00:05:56.079
particularly vulnerable, they encouraged their children to watch over the chickens and to

00:05:56.079 --> 00:06:02.519
hit pans with a metal spoon so that the resulting noise would succeed in driving away birds

00:06:02.519 --> 00:06:05.880
that were trying to seize the young chicks.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:12.200
None of these methods was 100% effective, so as a result the village people told us

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:17.959
that rather than just using one method, they were forced to use a combination for them

00:06:17.959 --> 00:06:20.239
to have any effect.

00:06:20.239 --> 00:06:26.119
And even so, these birds of prey remain a major threat to the chickens' survival and

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:30.239
cause considerable economic loss to farmers.

00:06:30.239 --> 00:06:37.239
So, we looked at the possibility of a longer-term solution to increasing the number of chickens

00:06:37.239 --> 00:06:39.040
in the village.

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:41.559
That is the end of Part 4.

00:06:41.559 --> 00:06:45.160
